Of course not. As the manual page explicitly states, the -C /dir performs
a chdir() to that directory. Its purpose to avoid including long absolute 
pathnames, not to include the pathnames (which is the usual behaviour
of tar without the -C flag).

>I am totally disappointed with this -C option and I can't see any benifit on
>using this stupid option at all.

Since you used it for the precise opposite of its purpose, I could
understand that. The benEfit of using that "stupid option" is to prevent
ugly hacks to get around having to include absolute paths when two
directories which need to be archived have no close common parent.


>Also, when I talked to DEC Support, the
>answer I got is something like you should have tested the command and options
>first before you use it and now you are on your own.

That sounds accurate. If you use a flag expecting it to do the opposite
of what it is documented to do, then support can't do a lot, can they?

It sounds like you used this flag to do a crucial backup. In that case,
yes, you *should* have tested the options first. Don't assume that
backups or archives work, that's gotta be rule #1 of a backup policy.

