|> Dirk Grunwald  <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> wrote:
|> }(as an aside - Digital would be well off selling source code to
|> }Universities for cheap. Solaris is a great example. Our sysops people
|> }don't like to stock any O/S that doesn't come with source code.)

In fact, Digital (Compaq!) DOES sell UNIX source code to universities
for cheap, and we have been doing that since the days of ULTRIX.

See the "Software Product Description" for Digital UNIX:
	ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/SPD/41-61-XX.txt

which lists these licenses:
 Education Source License/Distribution: QB-MT4BA-E8
 Education Update Source License/Distribution: QB-MT4BE-E8

You need to use the first part number for initial orders, and
the "update" part number for subsequent updates.

The list price for these part numbers, last time I checked
(about 6 months ago) was $1,236.00.  If you get this through
a research grant, the incremental cost is almost negligible.

The one drawback is that, because Digital UNIX includes
sources derived from both System V and OSF, you will need
several prerequisite source licenses as well.  See the SPD
for details.  The prerequisite source licenses from The Open
Group might cost a few thousand dollars more (for a university).

-Jeff



