Just as an experiement, try using System Contention Scope
threads, which area first supported in V4.0D.

Use

pthread_attr_t attr;

pthread_attr_init(&attr);
pthread_attr_setscope(&attr, PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM);
pthread_create(&tid, &attr, start, param);

Major performance improvements were made for
select() in V4.0, especially in V4.0D. You're
also running on a *completely* new pthreads
package, whose default behavior is to use
process scope threads. If you're mostly blocking
in the kernel for I/O, then system-scope threads
may help you out in avoiding some user-scheduling
overhead.

There are some fairly detailed discussions of 2-level
schedulers, esp the Digital UNIX one, over
in comp.programming.threads. Check it out with
DejaNews...


Ron Heiby wrote:

> -----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----
>
> Hi!
>
> While my firm was running Digital UNIX 3.2G, I ran several benchmark
> programs to attempt to determine a number of transactions per second that
> might be made through my system, and (more important) the relative speed
> of performing certain operations in different ways.
>
> The basic test is to have a client program and a server program that pass
> blocks of data back and forth to each other. In each case, the server
> establishes a socket connection on which to listen and the client
> establishes N connections to the server. Each connection has its own
> socket. The client picks a connection at random and sends 1000 bytes on
> it. The server receives these 1000 bytes and sends them back on the same
> socket on which they were received. When the client receives the 1000
> bytes back from the server, it repeats the process. Every second, the
> client displays statistical information, basically the number of
> transactions (round trips) it was able to accomplish in the last second,
> plus total and moving averages.
>
> There are different versions of the server. One version uses a single
> process and a single thread in that process, handling all N socket
> connections using select(2). Another creates a thread for each socket, and
> each thread does a blocking read on its socket, waiting for something to
> do. Another version of that threaded server (and modified client) use
> AF_LOCAL (AF_UNIX) sockets, instead of AF_INET sockets. In each test, both
> client and server are on the same machine.
>
> Each test is run for at least ten minutes with each of various numbers of
> socket connections (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2000).
> (Why 2000, rather than 2048, you ask? Well, the thread approach runs out
> of system memory on my 128 Meg machine before I reach 2048 under 3.2G.
> Under 4.0D, I run out of memory a bit sooner.) The tests are run when the
> system is otherwise (basically) idle. The tests are run for at least ten
> minutes, with the average throughput for the entire run used in reporting
> the results. During each test, the system has zero idle time.
>
> The system is a 133 MHz Alpha with 128 Meg of RAM. I have attempted to
> have minimal tunable parameter changes from whatever the defaults were on
> the two versions, and have attempted to have the same changes made on both
> versions. The tunable changes are: sem-mni=32; msg-mni=1024;
> max-proc-per-user=8000; maxusers=1024; sominconn=1024;
> name-cache-hash-size=1024; vm-mapentries=16384; vm-ubcseqpercent=20; and
> vm-vpagemax=65536.
>
> I had not expected the select(2) version use so much system cpu time at
> higher numbers of sockets, but (thinking about it) it does seem
> reasonable. I was surprised that in moving from 3.2G to 4.0D, the
> select(2) version showed an improvement in performance. At the 1024 socket
> level, we went from 83 to 131 transactions per second (TPS). In fact, in
> the new version, the select(2) approach has better TPS numbers than the
> thread approach until a crossover at approximately 32 sockets, above which
> the thread approach is still superior. In the 3.2G tests, there was no
> crossover, with select(2) always being inferior to the thread approach, by
> increasingly large margins as the number of sockets increased.
>
> The thread approach shows a marked reduction in throughput under 4.0D vs.
> the levels were were seeing under 3.2G. At the 1024 socket level, we went
> from 1191 to 640 TPS. Using AF_LOCAL sockets, the performance went from
> 2446 to 857 TPS.
>
> We are quite surprised and disappointed by the dramatic reduction in
> performance we are seeing with 4.0D vs. 3.2G. We are curious as to what
> the cause is, and whether there are any tunable parameter changes that
> might be of help in restoring the previous (higher) performance levels.
>
> If the answer is that the 3.2G implementation of threads and/or sockets
> was very buggy and that 4.0D does it properly (though at a price), I can
> understand that. As my C.S. professor (Dershem) told me many years ago,
> "Any fool can write a fast sort program, if it doesn't have to put them in
> the right order." So, I'd rather have correct than fast. If it's something
> else, I'd like to know that, too, particularly if there is a known problem
> with a fix available or soon to be available.
>
> Thank you very much!
>
