-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Phil Hodges <hodges@decnet.enet.dec.com> wrote:
>I wonder if using poll(2) instead of select(2) would make a
>significant difference?

Under 3.2G, poll(2) was slightly slower in my tests, until we got upwards
of 32 socket connections. From there out, it was almost the same level of
performance as select(2). I have not repeated all of the poll(2) version
testing under 4.0D, but a "spot check" of the 1024 socket level shows a
throughput very close to the new (slightly faster) socket(2) throughput.

Even with the much slower thread throughput I'm seeing, the thread
implementation is still about an order of magnitude better than the
socket(2) or poll(2) implementation.

I suspect that in my test, with a large number of consecutively numbered
socket descriptors, one would naturally not see much difference between
select(2) and poll(2). I have not done the test, but I would expect that
checking fds 0, 1, 2, 1022, and 1023 would be faster with poll(2) than
with select(2), as the latter needs to look at each of the bits being
passed, to see if it needs to check that fd.

