Personally, I think Dave Butenhof's Addison-Wesley book,
_Programming_with_POSIX_Threads_ gives you the
most bang for your buck. There's a good discussion
of contention scope in there.

The best discussions of scope has been over the last few months
in comp.programming.threads -- check back in time with
dejanews.com.

Digital threading people contend that PCS threads are the
"best" and should be used exclusivley, except for occasional exceptions,
usually involving I/O. There are ongoing efforts
to reduce the overhead of PCS threads and narrow the
performance gap for those special cases (like yours).
The next major release of Compaq's Digital UNIX (tm)
(probably called V5.0) should look a little better at least.
A rule of thumb is of thumb here is to start out with
PCS threads (as you did) and only fall back to SCS
threads when performance doesn't live up to expectations.
BTW, there's no way to make you "main" thread SCS,
so you'll always have at least on PCS thread in the mix

In the Solaris world, where I've been hanging out the last
6 months or so, SCS threads are used almost exclusively.
This came about because of implementation deficiencies in
the Solaris 2-level scheduler. Things have gotten a little
better in 2.6, but everyone still uses SCS threads. I know
I do.

