> 
> that I can login as root from another account on the same machine with
> the command su - but so that root-login is disabled from another
> machine?

(that was a little confusing)

All entries in /etc/securettys describe "trusted" login-capable devices.
Root logins are allowed from these. Entry for telnet/rlogin/ssh is
"ptys". Remove it and nobody can login as root from the network.

Command "su" is not a login. It is "set user" or "substitute user", you
do not login, you just gain privileges of another user. On Digital UNIX,
in order to su to another user, you and that user must have at least one
group in common. In other words, to su to root from account tom, tom
must be in group system.

Nix.




