> rz0g
> mkfdmn -r /dev/rz0a  r

!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

Ahhh. You are forgeting something (I am not, since I had a "privilege"
of restoring root file sistem from backup). You must make that disk
AdvFS bootable.

Tom asked if he could make partition h bootable. Well,...

For a partition to be bootable, it must have offset of 0. AFAIK, any
partition starting from 0 can be bootable, if you have a proper
bootstrap loader (I'm not sure if it looks at the label). But, tradition
is tradition, let us make it partition a.

Since there are 2 posible FSs for root, there are TWO bootstrap loaders.
Each is installed via disklabel command. The problem is, I couldn't make
it work with -R option (restore form a file). I had to zero label,
install new and then restore the old. So, supposing you have RZ29
disk...

# disklabel rz0 > /usr/disklabel.RZ0
# dikslabel -z rz0
# disklabel -wr -t advfs rz0 RZ29
# disklabel -R rz0 /usr/disklabel.RZ0

Of course, this is "open hart surgery" WITHOUT anaestethics. This
actually deletes and writes a disklabel on the disk with live file
systems, namelly root and usr. You could really mess things up this way.

My advice (and the way I did it) is:

1. backup root FS thoroughly, 2 or 3 copies.
2. boot from OS CD-ROM, get to System Maintainance/Root Shell
3. cd /dev ; ./MAKEDEVrz0
4. play with disklabels as above
5. create AdvFS for root
6. mount it
7. restore root from backup
8. ammend root (you need to change /etc/fdmns, /etc/fstab)


>         Did I say backup first, The wrong command at the wrong moment and kiss
> you data good buy!

Couldn't agree more.

Nix.





