Kjell Andresen wrote:
> 
> Hi!
> 
> I've installed a new Fast EtherWORKS pci 10/100 kit (DE500-BA) into an
> alphastation 400 with DUv4.0D and it worked right away.
> (Don't think I even had to boot with genvmunix)
<SNIP>
> When I put the card into a similar machine running DUv4.0B and I never
> got the NIC running.
<SNIP>
> As it is a timeconsuming task I wonder if anybody have experienced
> this trouble or are able to tell me wheter or not the DE500-BA is
> supported in DUv4.0B/DUv4.0D

Please use this only as a starting point...

Last year, I went from a DE435 to a DE500-AA (same as BA, just different
chipset) on my AlphaServer 2000 4/233 running DUNIX 4.0a . I had the
same problems (OS would not see/use the new card). Turns out there was a
setting change needed at the SRM console prompt which is not well
documented in the firware reference manual or the DE500 manual.

What you want to check out is the ew*0_mode setting. This tells the OS
which port on the NIC is being used. If you previously had a DE435, or
this is the first NIC for the machine, it is probably set to "AUI". Of
course, the DE500 does not have any "AUI" ports (10base5/10base2). The
setting you want for a DE500 running in 100Mbps mode is "Fast" (which is
not in the firmware manual.. Card made after book printed). To set it,
type at the ">>>":

	>>> show ew#
		Current settings displayed. Check out the "mode"!
	>>> set ew#0_mode "Fast"

Replace the "#" with the NIC letter. If it is the only NIC on the
machine, use "a". If it's the second NIC, use "b", and so on. Card order
is determined by location on the bus. It can usually be found out by
doing a "show device".

Do an "init" once set and bring up the system. Attempt to configure the
device when the system is up. If the device does not show, a
sizer/kernel rebuild may be necessary.

The other setting to try if "Fast" does not do it is "twisted-pair" (for
10Mbps mode perhaps??). That one IS in the firmware reference.

You may notice an "auto-sensing" setting in the manual. Don't use it!
The manual itself says it is "not implemented", and a firmware update
README I have states that it causes problems for machines with multiple
NICs.

Hope that helps...
Ed

