Derek Mallard wrote:
> 
> A friend of mine asked for some assistance with tightening up
> security for ftp access.  I think the answer is wuftpd.
> Anybody out there have some opinions on this?
> 
> The configuration:
> Digital UNIX V3.2G (Rev. 62); Sat Nov  9 11:19:24 MST 1996
> Digital UNIX V3.2G Worksystem Software (Rev. 62)
> 
> This is a web server/dns server/MX/ftp server/anon ftp server.
> 
> A concern has been raised about the ability to browse
> around directories when connected via ftp.
> The anonymous ftp can browse all of the anon ftp directory
> structure, of course.  I know of no way to prevent this.

A normal user connected via ftp can browse all the
directories from root on down.
Wu-ftpd is at my sense the best option: It compiles easily on du3.2g. To
bridle a real unix user in a directory, he has to belong to two groups:
the main one (in /etc/passwd) and another one that you specify in the
/etc/group file. The last one will be marked as guestgroup in the
ftpaccess file.

Take care of the wu-ftpd bugs: CERT Advisory CA-95:16 - wu-ftpd and CERT
Advisory CA-97.27 - FTP_bounce that you can get there: 
ftp://ftp.cert.org/pub/cert_advisories

Don't even try to make the users/groups names visible from the ftp
client: you have to recreate entirely the resolution structure
(/etc/sia, etc) needed by libc.a. And it's not "really" useful.

Hope it will help.

