Organization: Garetech Computer Solutions P/L
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hans Westerback wrote:
> 
> 
> Can some kind soul give me a hint on good commands to quickly get a feel
> for the disk setup,  I mean I'd like to know which disks there are on
> the system, which one are used by advfs and lsm and how.

Like most things on UNIX there are always serveral ways to get the
answer to any given 
question. 

When it comes to disks Digital UNIX showes more of its BSD side than
SYSV
The current naming scheme for SCSI based disks are /dev/rz* /dev/rrz*
The * may be either of two formats depending if you have SCSI LUNS
involved.
The simple case for a standalone SCSI disk on a non RAID bus is

/dev/rz followed by a number followed by the letter a-h
The number of represents the disk number (bus# * 8 + SCSIID) the letter
a-h
is a partition table entry for the drive.
eg /dev/rrz1h 
The partition table may exist in memory and or on the first blocks of
the disk,
by convention and default two partitions are based at block 0  a and c.
The c partition
is defaulted to the whole disk. Refer to /etc/disktab
	Use the disklabel command to read/write/edit the label with its
partition layout
There is a GUI /usr/sbin/diskconfig it does stupid things with size cals
(eg 1K = 1000)
and somewhat likes to start with a clean disk your mileage may vary :)

If all of the disk are configured properly and the device files created
in /dev
cd /dev
./MAKEDEV rz0 rz1 rz2 ...
then you can use file to find all of the disks
Your must do this as root to get the probe to work 
file /dev/rrz*c
# file /dev/rrz*c
/dev/rrz0c:     character special (8/2) SCSI #0 RZ28M disk #0 (SCSI ID
#0) (SCSI LUN #0)
/dev/rrz10c:    character special (8/18434) SCSI #1 RZ29B disk #80 (SCSI
ID #2) (SCSI LUN #0)
/dev/rrz1c:     character special (8/1026) SCSI #0 RZ28B disk #8 (SCSI
ID #1) (SCSI LUN #0)
/dev/rrz2c:     character special (8/2050) SCSI #0 RZ28M disk #16 (SCSI
ID #2) (SCSI LUN #0)
/dev/rrz4c:     character special (8/4098) SCSI #0 RRD45 disk #32 (SCSI
ID #4) (SCSI LUN #0)
/dev/rrz8c:     character special (8/16386) SCSI #1 RZ28D disk #64 (SCSI
ID #0) (SCSI LUN #0)

Also if you don't have an already configure system and need to find
them. You can
either look a the boot record which may tell you some of the disks uerf
-R -r 300 | more
or look in /var/adm/messages or Which will physically probe each
connected SCSI bus for devices
scu
scu> scan edt
Scanning all available buses, please be patient...
scu> show edt

CAM Equipment Device Table (EDT) Information:

    Device: RZ28M      Bus: 0, Target: 0, Lun: 0, Type: Direct Access
    Device: RZ28B      Bus: 0, Target: 1, Lun: 0, Type: Direct Access
    Device: RZ28M      Bus: 0, Target: 2, Lun: 0, Type: Direct Access
    Device: RRD45      Bus: 0, Target: 4, Lun: 0, Type: Read-Only Direct
Access
    Device: TLZ07      Bus: 0, Target: 5, Lun: 0, Type: Sequential
Access
    Device: RZ28D      Bus: 1, Target: 0, Lun: 0, Type: Direct Access
    Device: RZ29B      Bus: 1, Target: 2, Lun: 0, Type: Direct Access


df will quickly tell you about what file systems are mounted as advfs
(so will mount)
any advfs will have a device  of  domain#fileset eg.  root_domain#root	/
You can use showfdmn root_domain to check out the root domain
showfsets root_domain to see what file sets are in the given domain.
Also if you
look in /etc/fdmns any directory here is potentially a domain name (this
is the only
this that gives a domain a name no on disk structures or other
complexities)
so to rename a domain just mv the directory to another name in
/etc/fdmns
The structure next below this in /etc/fdmns just contains symbolic links
back to the partition
or lsm volume in /dev
ls -lag /etc/fdmns/usr_domain# ls -lag /etc/fdmns/usr_domain
total 16
drwxr-xr-x   2 root     system      8192 Feb 14 08:46 .
drwxr-xr-x   8 root     system      8192 Jan 16  1997 ..
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system         9 Jan 16  1997 rz0g -> /dev/rz0g
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system        10 Jan 17  1997 rz10e ->
/dev/rz10e
lrwxr-xr-x   1 root     system         9 Jan 16  1997 rz2h -> /dev/rz2h
lrwxr-xr-x   1 root     system         9 Jan 16  1997 rz8h -> /dev/rz8h

There are no hidden mysteries with this one. Fileset name are on disk
structure and you will 
need renamefset mkfset rmfset do manipulate filesets.
 
Files are mounted by /etc/fstab you may also what to check out the GUI
dtadvfs
You should also get sys_check from Digitals web page it does a good job
of canning a lot of these
commands together to produce a html file describing a system and will
also create /var/recovery
with all of the disk labels etc so that this is available in the backup
of the /var file system

> 
> But in Dunix with Advfs and LSM...

This is a few commands from the hip, if your also have LSM involved it
appears as another layer
between physical partitions and the filesystem. You may want to check
out dxlsm but most of all
get the manual on LSM and read it a few times then find somewhere to
test what you have learnt
in safety. LSM has two many ways of achieving a similar result and will
allow you to create
dangerous configs with out much or any warning that your standing to
close to the edge. This is
getting better each time they release a new version, but in anycase RTFM
this one, don't skip
out on the reading. Do a man on all of the command mentioned try running
them, good luck.


	Cheers
		mark :)



