Mark Bartelt wrote:
> 
> The version of libXt.{a,so} that's packaged with v4.0[bcd] of
> Digital UNIX is missing some routines that were present in v3:
> 
>         _XtActionInitialize
>         _XtInitErrorHandling
>         _XtR3StringToKeysym
>         _XtRegisterWindow
>         _XtUnregisterWindow

Functions with an '_' prefix are unspecified (i.e. undocumented)
internal functions and never should have been used by an application
program.


> This is a rather major nuisance for those of us who need to run
> executables that we don't have source code for, and which won't
> run if libXt.so doesn't have these things.

Which programs are these?

> 
> For now, I've (temporarily) replaced the v4 libXt stuff with the
> ones from v3.  But this seems like a bad hack.

Yup.

> Are these routines officially deprecated now?  

They were never officially sanctioned for public use; thus they did not
need to be deprecated -- officially or otherwise.

> And if so, what's
> the officially-sanctioned equivalent?  

R6 has XtRegisterDrawable and XtUnregisterDrawable which should suffice
for _XtRegisterWindow and _XtUnregisterWindow. There are no substitutes
for the other functions.


> (As I'm definitely not an
> X11 wizard, I'd *love* to get a canned "old X routine xyz is now
> replaced by new routine zyx, as follows:" sort of guide.  

Every release of X comes with a set of documentation. The Xt
specification includes a summary of the changes from one release to the
next; however you won't find the information you're looking for for
internal functions like _XtActionInitialize for the reasons I've
outlined above.

> If we
> had that, it would be easy to cobble together an "oldXkludge.so"
> that would solve our problem.)

No comment (retch, hack, gag, cough).

> 
> Or, are these routines missing because of some oversight out in
> DECland?

They're not missing, because for all practical purposes, they never
really existed. They never should have been used in the first place.

If some programmer has gone and done a symbol dump on libc.so and used
undocumented interfaces that he/she found, who should be blamed when the
next release changes or removes those interfaces? Hint: not Digital.


--
Kaleb

