In article <3576fc6d.0@ntserv.obu.edu>, "John Selph" <selphj@alpha.obu.edu> writes:
>-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----
>Hash: SHA1
>
>We're thinking of jumping to DEC Unix from VMS.  The machine is an
>internet server but mostly used for classwork.  Internet services will
>be better on Unix, so that part is not a worry.  But a couple of
>concerns:

Hm.  I do all the same internet things on both.  What in particular do
you need that someone hasn't ported yet?

>
>1.  Can Dec Unix read VMS format tapes?  Or should I look for
>something VMS that will write Unix tapes.

There's a program called vmsbackup.c out on the network somewhere, if
those "VMS format" tapes are VMS BACKUPs.  There are also three vendors
whocan sell you a comercial version.  There are several free tar
readers/writers out on the net for VMS.  Both VMS and DU can
read/write ANSI/ASCII labled tapes.  VMS calls these native Files-11
(not /foreign when you mount them) and DU provides ltf (user interface a
lot like tar).  And if you can deal with the details of the data, you
can exchange unlabled tapes via VMS COPY and DU dd.  You can even load
the POSIX kit on VMS and use tar, dd, and cpio.

>2.  Assembly will obviously not be the same.  But is there any kind of
>assembly in DEC Unix (Alpha) and if so of what flavor?  difficult to
>write programs in?  And remember I'm just talking enough to teach a
>class for.  I just need to know what to expect.

Macro-32 is the assembly language for VMS on a VAX (and for ULTRIX on a
VAX, but the assembler's a bit different), and a compiled
language for VMS on an Alpha.  Macro-64 is the assembly language on
Alpha, but again you'll probably find differences in the assemblers
between VMS (where it's an optional product) and DU.

