Stuart Grierson (grierson@ozemail.com.au) wrote:
: /bin/false, and editing the /etc/shells file.� This allows ftp, but not
: telnet.� The only problem that I can see with this is that they can't
: change their password� -� that and the ability to move around in ftp and
: grab sensitive files:)
: Does anyone have any ideas on how to set this sort of 'captive account'
: up??

Since the DU ftpd apparently does not support RESTRICT keyword in /etc/ftpusers,
you'll have to pick up an ftp server which does. wu-ftpd should do. Setting
/usr/bin/passwd as the login shell for the ftp-only users will allow them to
change their password. This however may create security problems on some unices 
(I do not know if this is true for DU), so to be on the safe side you may want
to make a small *statically* *linked* login wrapper which will clear the 
environment and exec /usr/bin/passwd (or yppasswd, if you use NIS).

Cheers,

/Serge.P



