A friend of mine asked for some assistance with tightening up
security for ftp access.  I think the answer is wuftpd.
Anybody out there have some opinions on this?

The configuration:
Digital UNIX V3.2G (Rev. 62); Sat Nov  9 11:19:24 MST 1996
Digital UNIX V3.2G Worksystem Software (Rev. 62)

This is a web server/dns server/MX/ftp server/anon ftp server.

A concern has been raised about the ability to browse
around directories when connected via ftp.
The anonymous ftp can browse all of the anon ftp directory
structure, of course.  I know of no way to prevent this.

A normal user connected via ftp can browse all the
directories from root on down.

The concern is that this allows a person to hack in
as a normal user and search through the system for 
other security holes.

The question:
Is there a way with the standard ftp daemon shipped with
Digital Unix 3.2 to prevent this?  I know how to restrict the
user to their home directory when they telnet in but not if
they ftp in.  The restriction on telnet really just protects
them from internal attacks since you cannot get to port 23 from
the Internet.

Regards,
Derek



